{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794783475,"nonce":"aMNAp+il4J+JiKafOFO8G0K9Lxo=","title":"A rows frame on the cn_demo page costs about 3.4 ms of CPU","message":"At rest at the bottom of cn_demo, three spinner turns and four skeleton pulses keep the window drawing at display rate (70-88 fps), every frame on rows. The process measured 45% CPU (Activity Monitor, user) with a symbolized release-debug build.\n\n`sample` of that state showed images drawn one render pass and one queue.submit each (`render_images` -\u003e `render_images_with_aux_blend`), half of the main thread's frame, with the Metal command queue thread busy on the submissions. Batching consecutive images into one pass (`GpuRenderer::render_image_set`) took the window to 24% (user's measurement).\n\nRemaining split at 24%, as shares of one core: main thread about 12% (re-uploading the moved content's primitives each frame about 2.5%: `upload_sorted_primitives` -\u003e `write_buffer`; the layered overlay path for the skeleton opacity groups, which clones the batch and applies rows on the CPU, about 2.7%; static composite about 1.6%; encoders, passes and submits the rest); Metal driver threads about 7%.\n\nFor reference, three spinners over a 600-node tree in tools/blinc-bench cn_progress_window (SPINNERS=3) cost 8-14% at 75-95 fps.\n\nNext:\n1. One encoder and one submit for a rows-only frame (static composite, dynamic overlay, motion pools).\n2. Keep the moved content's primitives resident in their own region of the primitive buffer, uploaded once per paint; a rows frame uploads only the node table. A byte comparison costs as much as the upload, so this needs a paint generation.\n3. A group opacity carried by rows should not send the batch through the layered overlay's CPU clone.","files":null}]}